---
description: Kotlin code style, naming, and structure for the OpenAPI Test Generator project
globs:
  - "*/src/main/kotlin/**/*.kt"
  - "*/src/test/kotlin/**/*.kt"
alwaysApply: true
---

You are a senior Kotlin developer working on a Gradle plugin project that generates tests from OpenAPI specs.

Follow these rules when writing or editing Kotlin code:

- Naming
  - Use PascalCase for classes and interfaces; camelCase for functions and variables; UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants.
  - Choose descriptive, intention-revealing names. Avoid abbreviations and 1–2 character names.
  - Prefer explicit argument names when constructing complex objects.

- Types and APIs
  - Annotate public APIs and exported types explicitly. Avoid `Any` and unchecked casts.
  - Favor immutable data structures and `data class` for value types.
  - Use early returns and guard clauses to reduce nesting; handle edge cases first.

- Error handling
  - Do not swallow exceptions. Either rethrow with context or handle meaningfully.
  - Prefer `require`/`check` for argument/state validation in constructors and public functions.

- Collections and null safety
  - Avoid `!!`. Use safe calls, `requireNotNull`, or null-object defaults.
  - Prefer pure transformation pipelines; keep side-effects localized.

- Formatting
  - Match the existing code style; keep lines reasonably short; break long argument lists across lines.
  - Write multi-line builders/DSL code with clear indentation and trailing commas where useful.
  - Preserve indentation style per file; do not mix tabs and spaces.

- Logging
  - Use SLF4J with parameterized messages. Do not build strings via concatenation in logs.

- Tests
  - Use JUnit 5. Keep tests deterministic and focused; name tests by behavior.
  - Avoid network and file I/O in unit tests unless explicitly controlled under `core/src/test/resources`.

- Comments
  - Keep comments concise and focused on the "why". Avoid restating code.

Do not reformat unrelated code. Keep edits minimal and high-signal.

